m_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"I can't see the bank's reviewed agreements, so I'm answering from standard US ETF Authorized Participant (AP) agreement and Statement of Additional Information (SAI) practice. Check the figures against your review.\n\n- **Collateral required:** When an AP is allowed to create before delivering all deposit securities, it must post cash collateral. This is typically **up to 115%** of the shortfall. Some sponsors specify a range, such as at least 105% and up to 115%. A few go higher, around 125%.\n- **What the percentage applies to:** The **market value of the undelivered (missing) deposit securities**, marked to market daily. If the value rises, the AP must post more collateral. If the AP fails to deliver, the fund can use the cash to buy the securities, and the AP pays any shortfall and costs.\n- **Is it universal?** No. The rate varies by sponsor, fund and agreement. Each fund's AP agreement and SAI set the actual terms, and the issuer can usually adjust them.","ttft_ms":5369,"type":"result","duration_ms":8588,"uuid":"693be0f8-49a7-4c7c-b1ad-373278ea5b69","ttft_stream_ms":1708,"time_to_request_ms":57,"first_content_frame_ms":1708,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
